  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nMontana continues to be under the influence of an upper level \nridge today, creating another day of warm temperatures and dry \nconditions. While these warm temperatures will last through \nSunday, shortwaves embedded in the upper level ridge and an \nincrease in monsoonal moisture will bring chances for showers and \nthunderstorms tomorrow through the weekend. Thunderstorms will \nmainly impact Southwest MT tomorrow night, with a transition to \nmore stratiform precipitation Saturday morning. Saturday evening \nwill see chances for thunderstorms in Southwest and Central MT, \nalthough isolated convection may make its way up to the Hi-Line. \nSunday afternoon into the evening, thunderstorm chances increase \nto include Southwest, Central and North-Central MT. The main \nthreat with these thunderstorms will be gusty winds, small hail, \nfrequent lightning and brief, heavy downpours. While conditions \nare currently not favorable for strong to severe storms this \nweekend, we cannot rule out the possibility of an isolated one \nforming. \n\nThe upper level ridge will begin to break down on Sunday as an \nupper level trough moves over the Northern Rockies from the west. \nThis will bring breezy conditions across the CWA on Sunday. A cold\nfront moving through Sunday night will decrease temperatures to \nbelow seasonal averages Monday and Tuesday. However, another upper\nlevel ridge building over Montana will cause temperatures to rise\nagain on Wednesday. This rise in temperatures will be short lived\nas an upper level trough moves west once again, lowering \ntemperatures and bringing back chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. -Dzomba\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main source of uncertainty in this forecast is the timing of \nthe ridge break down and subsequent cold front. This timing will \ndetermine when we see the highest non-convective winds. Currently,\nmodels are showing the ridge beginning to break down Sunday \nafternoon, which coincide with the peak of the breezy conditions.\nIf this ridge break down and cold front arrive earlier or later, \nthe timing of the gusty winds will follow suit.\n\nThunderstorms:\nThere is a 15-20% chance of thunderstorms in Southwest MT tomorrow\nnight into early Saturday morning. On Saturday, there is a 25-30%\nchance of thunderstorms in Southwest MT, and a 15-20% chance of\nthunderstorms in Central MT. North-Central MT will see low, but\nnon-zero, chances of thunderstorms Saturday, generally below 15%.\nOn Sunday, there is a 25-40% chance of thunderstorms in Southwest\nand Central MT, and a 15-20% chance of thunderstorms in North-Central\nMT. Over the weekend. The inflow of monsoonal moisture will \nincrease precipitable water to over an inch, creating the risk of \nlocalized flash flooding over burn scars with a stronger \nthunderstorm. The potential for flash flooding will depend on how \nfast the thunderstorm is moving, and what the rainfall rates \nassociated with it are. \n\nPrecipitation:\nSaturday through Sunday, there is a 50-70% chance of at least a\nwetting rain (0.1 inches) in Southwest MT. This chance decreases\nto a 15-30% chance in Central MT, and a 5-15% chance in North-Central\nMT. For a quarter of an inch during this same time period, there \nis a 15-25% chance in the valleys and a 35-50% chance in the \nmountains of Southwest MT. This chance decreases to less than 5% \nacross Central and North-Central MT. \n\nWind:\nOn Saturday, there is a greater than 50% chance for wind gusts \ngreater than 25 mph throughout the CWA. For wind gusts greater \nthan 30 mph, this chance remains greater than 50% east of a line \nbetween Havre and Lewistown, and south of Dillon. For the rest of \nthe CWA, this chance decreases to 20-45%. For wind gusts greater \nthan 40 mph, the only considerable chance will be in northern \nBlaine County, with a 50-75% chance. \n\nOn Sunday, there is a greater than 75% chance of wind gusts \ngreater than 25 mph across the entire CWA. For wind gusts greater \nthan 30 mph, there is a 65-85% chance in Southwest MT, a 50-80% \nchance in Central MT, and a 45-65% chance in North-Central MT, \nwith the exception of northern Blaine County and along the Rocky \nMountain front, in which chances are 80-100%. For wind gusts \ngreater than 40 mph, these chances decrease to 25-50% in \nSouthwest MT, 15-30% in Central MT, 15-30% along the Hi-Line east \nof Chester, 20-50% along the Hi-Line west of Chester to Cut Bank, \nand 50-70% along the Rocky Mountain front. There is a 25-50% \nchance of wind gusts greater than 50 mph along the Rocky Mountain \nfront. -Dzomba",
